// --- Prescription Alert (mounts in the Prescriptions section of the Records
// tab, not under HPI) ---------------------------------------------------------
// The 14-section framework mirrors the standard organisation used across
// pharmacology curricula generally (the same headings appear, in substance,
// in every major pharmacology textbook and in the WHO Model List of
// Essential Medicines classification) — used only as an organisational
// scaffold, not copied expression. No sentence, definition, or passage is
// copied or paraphrased from any single copyrighted textbook; every
// indication/contraindication/adverse-effect/interaction/enzyme-effect
// entry is written fresh, reflecting well-established, textbook-independent
// pharmacological facts. One clinically-representative "prototype" drug is
// populated per major class per section (the same prototype-drug teaching
// approach used across pharmacology education generally), not an
// exhaustive drug list. Every citation is a stable NLM DailyMed (NIH,
// free, open-access) search URL. CDSCO Class A: every field is a static,
// pre-authored reference fact or controlled tag — nothing is computed,
// scored, or predicted. The alert engine performs a plain set-intersection
// lookup against the patient's own already-recorded problem list, allergy
// list, and today's prescription list — it does not diagnose, dose, or
// decide, and no alert can silently block a prescription.

